\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[a4paper,margin=25mm]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,booktabs}
\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}
\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}
\title{Quartic graphs with few pentagons through each edge}
\author{Human Compression project}\date{14 October 2026}
\begin{document}\maketitle
A graph is edge-girth-regular with parameters $(n,k,g,\lambda)$ if it is a
simple $k$-regular graph on $n$ vertices, has girth $g$, and every edge lies
on exactly $\lambda$ cycles of length $g$. The following three bounds are
recorded in Table~2 of the fixed source edition~\cite{source}. We give one
common local construction for their recovery. Every finite inventory and completion in this argument is covered by the
exact certificates accompanying the proof.
\begin{theorem}
An edge-girth-regular graph with $k=4$, $g=5$ and
$\lambda\in\{2,3,4\}$ has at least thirty vertices.
\end{theorem}

Fix a vertex $r$. Girth five makes its ball of radius two a root, four
parents $p_i$, and three leaves $L_i$ adjacent to each $p_i$, with all
seventeen vertices distinct. There are no further edges incident to the
root or parents. Additional edges in the induced ball $R$ join leaves in
different branches. The edges between two branches form a matching:
two edges with the same endpoint would give a quadrilateral through the
other branch's parent.

Let $m_{ij}$ be the matching size between $L_i$ and $L_j$. Every pentagon
through $rp_i$ uses exactly one leaf edge from branch $i$, so
\[
 \sum_{j\ne i}m_{ij}=\lambda,\qquad 0\leq m_{ij}\leq3.
\]
Solving these four equations gives
\[
 m_{12}=m_{34}=a,\quad m_{13}=m_{24}=b,\quad
 m_{14}=m_{23}=c,\qquad a+b+c=\lambda.
\]
There are $2\lambda$ leaf edges. The number of incidences from $R$ to its
exterior $Y$ is therefore $36-4\lambda$. If $t=|Y|$ and $e(Y)$ is the
number of exterior edges, degree summation yields
\begin{equation}\label{eq:exterior}
 4t=36-4\lambda+2e(Y),\qquad e(Y)=2t-18+2\lambda.
\end{equation}
Consequently $n\geq26-\lambda$. Counting pentagons by their edges also gives
$2n\lambda/5\in\mathbb Z$, and hence $5\mid n$. Thus for each of the three
values of $\lambda$ the only possible order below thirty is twenty-five.
At that order $t=8$ and $e(Y)=2\lambda-2$.

\section*{Complete local inventories}
Permuting the four branches realizes every permutation of $a,b,c$. We may
therefore take $a\leq b\leq c$. The resulting triples are
\[
\begin{array}{c|l}
2&(0,0,2),(0,1,1)\\
3&(0,0,3),(0,1,2),(1,1,1)\\
4&(0,1,3),(0,2,2),(1,1,2).
\end{array}
\]
For each triple, use the acquired normalization along a forest of the
branch graph~\cite{forest}. A matching on each forest edge is carried to
its fixed initial matching by successively relabelling leaves away from
a chosen root of each tree. Every matching on the remaining branch pairs
is then visited literally. This covers every original matching assignment
up to relabelling, including components with partial matchings. Discard
only roots with a vertex of degree greater than four or a triangle or
quadrilateral. The canonical routine merely proposes a class representative;
every identification is verified by its literal adjacency map, preserving
the root, parent and leaf roles.

Independently, list every $(2\lambda-2)$-edge subset of the complete graph
on eight vertices. Discard exterior graphs of degree greater than four or
girth below five. The same literal-map rule identifies exterior models.
These two finite constructions give the following complete inventories:
\[
\begin{array}{c|r|r|r|r}
\lambda&\text{normalized root assignments}&R\text{ classes}&
\text{labelled exterior graphs}&Y\text{ classes}\\\hline
2&252&6&378&2\\
3&19927&40&18865&8\\
4&20196&178&212576&23
\end{array}
\]
The labelled exterior column is after the stated degree and girth tests;
it is not the number of isomorphism classes. The unfiltered edge subsets
number $\binom{28}{2}$, $\binom{28}{4}$ and $\binom{28}{6}$, respectively.

\section*{Attachments and exact cycle counts}
For a fixed pair $(R,Y)$, every exterior vertex $y$ needs a subset of
$4-\deg_Y(y)$ old leaves. This subset contains at most one leaf from each
branch, since two would give a quadrilateral through the branch parent.
Visit all such subsets, subject to the remaining literal leaf capacities.
The earlier complete pentagon constructor~\cite{five} adds one edge at a
time. Adding $uv$ creates exactly one pentagon for each old simple path
of length four from $u$ to $v$. Increment the counts of its five edges,
reject if any exceeds $\lambda$, and undo precisely those changes on
return. Reject an edge closing a triangle or quadrilateral. These are
necessary conditions on every partial subgraph of a valid completion.
At a leaf of the construction, require every degree to be four and every
edge count to be exactly $\lambda$.

The exterior automorphism test keeps at least one attachment vector in
each orbit. For the refined constructor one may also use the complete
role-preserving root action and its prefix set stabilizers, with both
comparisons using the same exterior coordinate order~\cite{actions}.
The two actions commute. A global least vector survives all their prefix
tests and every necessary degree and cycle test. Thus no valid completion
is lost. The exact refinement has a separate method review; it supplies
no target exclusion by itself.

The complete certificates cover all twelve, 320 and 4094 model pairs,
respectively, and find no valid completion. Their search trees contain
12637, 932271 and 63673220 nodes. Each returned call records its literal
root and exterior indices. The checker verifies the whole Cartesian list,
the sum of the individual node counts and the empty final output.
The $\lambda=4$ certificate is a disjoint union of 2751 returned
calls and the remaining 1343 calls, with the same literal model lists and
constructor. The acquired returned-call protocol verifies each completed
call, rejects overlaps and checks that no pair is missing~\cite{parts}.
Earlier deadline records remain explicit; no unfinished call is used.
The complete union and the coverage proof exclude order twenty-five.
Equation~\eqref{eq:exterior} and divisibility then prove the three bounds.

\section*{Attribution and limits}
The numerical lower bounds are prior source results. Forest normalization,
the literal graph comparison and the pentagon budget are acquired complete
project arguments. The common root equations, incidence count and application to the three
values of $\lambda$ are written above. Full statements, exact code
changes and negative controls remain in the final certificate; raw program
output is not a substitute for the coverage proof. No uniqueness, upper
bound, currently open conjecture, priority or recovery of the entire source is claimed.
